<article class="prose">
  <section id="scope" aria-labelledby="scope-title">
    <h2 id="scope-title">What this runbook covers</h2>
    <p>
      You run Postgres with a logical-decoding CDC connector (this page uses
      Debezium's Postgres connector as the example). The disk on the primary is
      filling, or an alert says it might, and you need to know whether a
      replication slot is the cause, what each fix costs, and what the pipeline
      owes you afterwards.
    </p>
    <p>
      The thread through all of it is the same as the rest of this site. A slot
      is a <em>position held on the source</em>. The consumer moves that position
      forward only by confirming it has received changes, so delivery is
      at-least-once. Anything that gives up or loses the position (a drop, an
      invalidation) means the only correct way back is a re-snapshot into a sink
      that is idempotent: keyed by primary key and ordered by log position. There
      is no end-to-end exactly-once at any point in this runbook.
    </p>
    <aside class="callout" aria-label="Versions checked">
      <p>
        <strong>Checked against:</strong> the PostgreSQL documentation for
        versions 14, 16, 17 and 18 (the supported releases as of 2026-10-09; the
        docs for 15 were not read separately), and the Debezium PostgreSQL
        connector reference at <code>debezium.io/documentation/reference/stable</code>,
        which was labelled 3.7 when read. Where behaviour differs by version the
        text says so, and where I could not confirm something it says
        &ldquo;check your version&rdquo;. The last section lists what was run
        and what was not.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section id="triage" aria-labelledby="triage-title">
    <h2 id="triage-title">Triage: is a slot why the disk is filling?</h2>
    <p>
      Connect to the primary (not a replica) and look at every slot, logical and
      physical. <code>pg_current_wal_lsn()</code> cannot run during recovery, so
      on a standby use <code>pg_last_wal_replay_lsn()</code> in its place.
    </p>
    <pre><code>-- Every slot, biggest WAL pin first. Needs PostgreSQL 13+ (wal_status, safe_wal_size).
-- A lost slot pins nothing, so its restart_lag is shown as NULL and it sorts last.
SELECT slot_name,
       slot_type,
       plugin,
       database,
       active,
       active_pid,
       wal_status,
       pg_size_pretty(safe_wal_size) AS safe_wal_size,
       pg_size_pretty(CASE WHEN wal_status &lt;&gt; 'lost'
                           THEN pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), restart_lsn) END) AS restart_lag,
       pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), confirmed_flush_lsn)) AS unconfirmed,
       restart_lsn,
       confirmed_flush_lsn
FROM pg_replication_slots
ORDER BY CASE WHEN wal_status &lt;&gt; 'lost'
              THEN pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), restart_lsn) END DESC NULLS LAST;</code></pre>
    <table>
      <caption>How to read the columns</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Column</th>
          <th scope="col">What it tells you</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>active</code>, <code>active_pid</code></th>
          <td>
            <code>true</code> means a session is streaming from the slot right
            now, and <code>active_pid</code> is its process. <code>false</code>
            with a NULL pid means <em>nobody is connected</em>. The slot keeps
            pinning WAL anyway.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>restart_lsn</code></th>
          <td>
            The oldest WAL that might still be required by the consumer. The
            server will not remove WAL from here onward, unless a
            <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> limit is exceeded. NULL if no
            WAL was ever reserved.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>confirmed_flush_lsn</code></th>
          <td>
            How far the consumer has confirmed receiving. Data from
            transactions that committed before it is not available anymore from
            the slot. (Logical slots only; NULL for physical slots.) After a
            server crash the slot can still go back to an earlier position (see
            below).
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>restart_lag</code></th>
          <td>
            Current WAL position minus <code>restart_lsn</code> (a byte count,
            shown with <code>pg_size_pretty</code> in the query). For a slot that
            is not <code>lost</code>, this is the WAL it makes the server keep:
            an estimate of what the slot pins, not the size of
            <code>pg_wal</code>. A <code>lost</code> slot pins nothing, whatever
            this column would show, so the query returns NULL for it.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>unconfirmed</code></th>
          <td>
            Current position minus <code>confirmed_flush_lsn</code>: how much
            the consumer has not yet confirmed. Sample twice to separate a slow
            consumer from a stopped one (see the decision table).
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>wal_status</code></th>
          <td>
            <code>reserved</code>: the WAL the slot needs is within
            <code>max_wal_size</code>. <code>extended</code>: beyond
            <code>max_wal_size</code> but still retained, either by the slot or
            by <code>wal_keep_size</code> (so it is not proof of a slot problem
            on its own). <code>unreserved</code>:
            the slot no longer retains the WAL it needs and some will be removed
            at the next checkpoint (it can return to <code>reserved</code> or
            <code>extended</code>). <code>lost</code>: the slot is no longer
            usable. For WAL retention, the last two states normally appear only
            when <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> is non-negative; newer
            releases can also invalidate a slot for other reasons.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>safe_wal_size</code></th>
          <td>
            Bytes that can still be written before the slot is in danger of
            becoming <code>lost</code>. NULL for lost slots, and NULL whenever
            <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> is <code>-1</code>.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="muted small">
      Careful with <code>lost</code> slots: a lost slot pins nothing, whatever
      <code>restart_lag</code> would show. With
      <code>invalidation_reason = 'wal_removed'</code> its
      <code>restart_lsn</code> is NULL (observed, see the last section), but
      with other reasons (for example <code>idle_timeout</code> on PostgreSQL
      18) it can stay set. A NULL lag therefore means &ldquo;this slot has
      already failed&rdquo;, not &ldquo;nothing is retained by design&rdquo;.
      Read <code>wal_status</code> (and, on 17+, <code>invalidation_reason</code>)
      before the lag columns.
    </p>
    <p>
      Next, confirm the disk is really WAL, and look for the other thing a slot
      pins. Reading <code>pg_ls_waldir()</code> needs a superuser or a role with
      <code>pg_monitor</code> privileges.
    </p>
    <pre><code>-- Size of the WAL directory
SELECT pg_size_pretty(sum(size)) AS pg_wal_size FROM pg_ls_waldir();

-- The same slots also hold back VACUUM on catalog rows
SELECT slot_name,
       age(xmin)         AS xmin_age,
       age(catalog_xmin) AS catalog_xmin_age
FROM pg_replication_slots;

-- PostgreSQL 17+ only: when did it go idle, and was it invalidated?
SELECT slot_name, inactive_since, invalidation_reason, failover, synced
FROM pg_replication_slots;</code></pre>
    <p>
      If <code>pg_wal_size</code> is far larger than <code>max_wal_size</code>
      and a slot's <code>restart_lag</code> accounts for most of it, a slot is
      the cause. If no slot explains it, look at WAL archiving and at physical
      slots for standbys before blaming CDC (the first query lists those too).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-slots-work" aria-labelledby="how-slots-work-title">
    <h2 id="how-slots-work-title">How a logical slot holds WAL</h2>
    <p>
      A logical slot represents a stream of changes that can be replayed to a
      client in the order they were made. It lives on one database, persists
      independently of whichever connection is using it, and is crash-safe.
      Crucially it <em>knows nothing about its consumer</em>. Only one receiver
      can consume from it at a time, but the server does not care whether that
      receiver is alive.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        The consumer connects and starts streaming. The server begins at the
        greater of the position the client asks for and the slot's
        <code>confirmed_flush_lsn</code>, so a client cannot read behind its own
        confirmed position.
      </li>
      <li>
        As it processes changes, the consumer sends status updates that report
        the WAL position it has flushed. That is how
        <code>confirmed_flush_lsn</code> moves forward. <code>restart_lsn</code>
        follows, possibly a little behind it, and that is what lets the server
        recycle older WAL. (From PostgreSQL internals rather than a docs
        sentence I can cite: in practice a long-running open transaction can
        hold <code>restart_lsn</code> back, because decoding must be able to
        restart from the start of every transaction not yet decoded.)
      </li>
      <li>
        If the consumer stops confirming, nothing else moves. Both positions
        stay where they were, and every new WAL file written from
        <code>restart_lsn</code> onward is kept.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Two details matter for the rest of this page. First, the slot's current
      position is persisted only at checkpoints, so after a server crash the
      slot can return to an earlier position and recent changes are sent again;
      the PostgreSQL docs say logical decoding clients are responsible for
      handling the same message more than once. Second, the confirmation is
      about <em>receipt by the consumer</em>. It says nothing about whether the
      sink applied the change.
    </p>
    <aside class="callout" aria-label="wal_keep_size is not the slot cap">
      <p>
        <strong><code>wal_keep_size</code> does not cap a slot.</strong> It sets
        the <em>minimum</em> amount of past WAL kept in case a standby needs to
        fetch it for streaming replication. The setting that limits what
        replication slots may retain is <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code>,
        covered below. (Debezium's PostgreSQL reference says you can set
        <code>wal_keep_size</code> &ldquo;to limit the maximum WAL size of a
        replication slot&rdquo;. That does not match the PostgreSQL
        documentation for <code>wal_keep_size</code>, which I follow here.)
        Glossary:
        <a href="{{ '/glossary/#log-retention' | url }}">log retention</a>,
        <a href="{{ '/glossary/#lsn-scn' | url }}">LSN</a>.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section id="why-it-fills" aria-labelledby="why-it-fills-title">
    <h2 id="why-it-fills-title">Why an unconsumed or inactive slot fills the disk</h2>
    <p>
      The PostgreSQL docs put it bluntly: replication slots persist across
      crashes and prevent removal of required resources <em>even when there is
      no connection using them</em>. The WAL is not recycled, so the disk fills
      at the rate the database writes. The usual routes into that state:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The connector is stopped, paused, crash-looping or deleted but
        its slot remains.</strong> With Debezium's defaults the slot survives a
        graceful stop (<code>slot.drop.on.stop</code> is <code>false</code>), so
        a connector you shut down on Friday is still pinning WAL on Monday.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The connector is running but slower than the write rate.</strong>
        The slot is active, <code>unconfirmed</code> keeps growing, and
        <code>restart_lsn</code> trails.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Most of the database's writes are not on captured tables.</strong>
        The connector reads the log as usual but rarely emits a change record,
        so no offset is committed and it never gets the chance to send the
        latest LSN back. The database keeps WAL for events the connector has
        already read. Debezium documents this case and the heartbeat fix below.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The captured database is the quiet one in a busy cluster.</strong>
        Slots are per database, but WAL is shared by all databases in the
        cluster. If only another database is busy, the connector is never
        invoked, cannot confirm a position, and WAL grows until the captured
        database emits an event.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Managed idle environments.</strong> Debezium's docs note that on
        AWS RDS for PostgreSQL, writes to RDS's own system tables that are
        invisible to clients can leave a nominally idle database in the same
        situation. Regular events solve it. Check your provider's behaviour.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The slot is abandoned.</strong> A test connector, a retired
        pipeline, a trial of a managed ELT product. Nobody remembers it exists.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      WAL is not the only cost. The same caution in the PostgreSQL docs says a
      slot also prevents <code>VACUUM</code> from removing the catalog rows it
      still needs, and that in extreme cases the database can shut down to
      prevent transaction ID wraparound. That is why the triage includes
      <code>age(catalog_xmin)</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cap" aria-labelledby="cap-title">
    <h2 id="cap-title">Capping retention: <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code></h2>
    <p>
      <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> is the maximum size of WAL that
      replication slots are allowed to retain in <code>pg_wal</code> at
      checkpoint time. The default is <code>-1</code>, which means
      <strong>unlimited</strong>: slots may retain any amount of WAL. A value
      without units is read as megabytes. The setting exists in PostgreSQL 13 and
      later and the docs for 13, 16, 17 and 18 describe it the same way.
    </p>
    <p>
      With a limit set, if a slot's <code>restart_lsn</code> falls behind the
      current position by more than the limit, the server stops protecting that
      WAL. The slot moves through <code>unreserved</code> to <code>lost</code>,
      and the consumer can no longer continue from it because the WAL it
      needs has been removed. The docs word it as the consumer possibly
      &ldquo;no longer [being] able to continue replication due to removal of
      required WAL files&rdquo;. In PostgreSQL 17 and later
      <code>invalidation_reason</code> says <code>wal_removed</code>.
    </p>
    <h3>What a limit trades</h3>
    <p>
      A limit trades <strong>disk for a lost slot</strong>. With no limit, a stuck
      consumer can fill the volume and stop the primary from writing, which
      takes the application down with it. With a limit, the primary survives, and the cost
      moves to the pipeline: the slot is invalidated and the only correct
      recovery is a re-snapshot. Both are real costs. A limit decides which one
      you pay.
    </p>
    <p>
      So size it as policy, not as a number copied from a blog post: it should be
      <strong>large enough to cover the longest consumer outage you intend to
      survive at your peak WAL rate, and small enough that the volume still has
      headroom</strong> for normal WAL, checkpoints and archiving. Both inputs
      (write rate, disk size) are yours. Treat <code>safe_wal_size</code> as the
      countdown to <code>lost</code> and alert well before it reaches zero.
    </p>
    <h3>Newer safety nets, by version</h3>
    <table>
      <caption>Version differences that matter for slot health</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">PostgreSQL</th>
          <th scope="col">What is new</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">13+</th>
          <td>
            <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code>, <code>wal_status</code>,
            <code>safe_wal_size</code>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">17+</th>
          <td>
            <code>inactive_since</code> and <code>invalidation_reason</code> in
            <code>pg_replication_slots</code>; the <code>failover</code> and
            <code>synced</code> columns; failover slots (see below).
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">18+</th>
          <td>
            <code>idle_replication_slot_timeout</code>: invalidates a slot that
            has been inactive longer than the duration. The default is zero,
            which disables it, and invalidation happens at a checkpoint. It
            shows up as <code>invalidation_reason = 'idle_timeout'</code>. It
            only helps if you want an abandoned slot to be invalidated
            automatically. An idle slot that is invalidated this way is lost,
            with the same recovery as any other lost slot.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="muted small">
      The docs say <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> can only be set in
      <code>postgresql.conf</code> or on the server command line. On PostgreSQL
      18.3, <code>pg_settings.context</code> for it is <code>sighup</code>, which
      means a reload applies it; check the same column on your version and on a
      managed service, where the setting may live in a parameter group.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="debezium" aria-labelledby="debezium-title">
    <h2 id="debezium-title">Debezium specifics</h2>
    <table>
      <caption>Debezium PostgreSQL connector settings that decide slot health</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Setting</th>
          <th scope="col">Default</th>
          <th scope="col">What it does for this problem</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>slot.name</code></th>
          <td><code>debezium</code></td>
          <td>
            The slot the connector streams from; created automatically if
            missing (the user needs the <code>REPLICATION</code> privilege, and
            with <code>pgoutput</code> also <code>CREATE</code> on the
            database). <strong>Every connector instance needs its own slot
            name</strong> and its own publication. Debezium warns that if
            connectors share a slot, each change reaches only one of them, with
            no warning from Postgres. Names are lower-case letters, digits and
            underscore.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>slot.drop.on.stop</code></th>
          <td><code>false</code></td>
          <td>
            Drop the slot when the connector stops gracefully. Debezium says to
            set it to <code>true</code> only in testing or development, because
            dropping the slot lets the database discard WAL and the connector
            then has to snapshot again or continue from a stored offset that the
            new slot cannot honour. In production the default is what you want,
            and the consequence is that <strong>decommissioning a connector
            means dropping its slot yourself</strong>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>heartbeat.interval.ms</code></th>
          <td><code>0</code> (off)</td>
          <td>
            A positive value makes the connector emit heartbeat messages, which
            gives it the chance to send the latest LSN to the database so old WAL
            can be reclaimed. Needed when many writes happen in the database but
            few touch the captured tables. Debezium also says heartbeats can
            reduce the number of change events re-sent when the connector
            restarts.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>heartbeat.action.query</code></th>
          <td>none</td>
          <td>
            A statement the connector runs on the source at each heartbeat. For
            a quiet database in a busy cluster, create a heartbeat table and
            point this at an <code>INSERT</code> (or repeated <code>UPDATE</code>)
            on it, so there are always captured changes to confirm. The
            connector's database user must be able to run it.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>publication.name</code>, <code>publication.autocreate.mode</code></th>
          <td><code>dbz_publication</code>, <code>all_tables</code></td>
          <td>
            With <code>pgoutput</code>, a publication defines which tables'
            changes are sent. <strong>The heartbeat table must be in the
            publication</strong> or the connector never sees the heartbeat
            writes. If the publication predates your deployment and is not
            <code>FOR ALL TABLES</code>, add it explicitly. <strong>In
            <code>filtered</code> mode the connector rewrites the publication
            from your include and exclude lists</strong>: Debezium documents
            that it runs <code>ALTER PUBLICATION &lt;name&gt; SET TABLE</code>
            for the tables matching those lists. So add
            <code>cdc_heartbeat</code> to <code>table.include.list</code>, not
            only to the publication. (That the manual
            <code>ADD TABLE</code> would be undone at the next start is my
            inference from the documented <code>SET TABLE</code>; I did not run
            it.)
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>lsn.flush.mode</code></th>
          <td><code>connector</code></td>
          <td>
            Who confirms the LSN to the slot. <code>manual</code> means the
            connector does not flush at all, and the docs warn WAL accumulates
            unless you build something that does. Newer releases only (it
            replaces <code>flush.lsn.source</code>), and
            <code>connector_and_driver</code> is documented as Technology
            Preview: check your version before relying on either.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <pre><code>-- Heartbeat table for a quiet database (the connector user needs rights on it)
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS cdc_heartbeat (id int PRIMARY KEY, beat timestamptz);
ALTER PUBLICATION dbz_publication ADD TABLE cdc_heartbeat;  -- only for a publication that is not FOR ALL TABLES
-- With publication.autocreate.mode=filtered, also list public.cdc_heartbeat in table.include.list</code></pre>
    <pre><code>{
  "heartbeat.interval.ms": "10000",
  "heartbeat.action.query": "INSERT INTO cdc_heartbeat (id, beat) VALUES (1, now()) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET beat = EXCLUDED.beat"
}</code></pre>
    <p class="muted small">
      The interval above is an example, not a recommendation: pick it from how
      long you can tolerate WAL being pinned on a quiet database. The queries
      and snippets on this page were checked for syntax where noted in the last
      section; the connector JSON was not run against a live Debezium.
    </p>
    <h3>What happens to <code>confirmed_flush_lsn</code> while the connector is down</h3>
    <p>
      Nothing. It stays at the last position the connector confirmed, and
      <code>restart_lsn</code> stays with it, so WAL accumulates for the whole
      outage. Heartbeats do not help here, because there is no connector to send
      them. When the connector restarts it asks for its stored offset, and the
      server streams from the greater of that and the slot's
      <code>confirmed_flush_lsn</code>. Changes the connector had already
      published to Kafka but not yet confirmed come around again. Debezium
      states this plainly: it provides at-least-once delivery and a recovering
      connector might emit duplicates.
    </p>
    <aside class="callout" aria-label="Heartbeats move the position, nothing more">
      <p>
        <strong>Heartbeats move the position for quiet tables. They do not make
        delivery exactly-once.</strong> They shrink the window of changes that
        can be replayed after a restart and let the server free WAL. The sink
        still has to expect duplicates.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section id="failover" aria-labelledby="failover-title">
    <h2 id="failover-title">Failover and standbys</h2>
    <p>
      A slot lives on one server. What happens to it when the primary fails
      depends on the PostgreSQL version.
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>Slots across a primary failure, by PostgreSQL version</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Version</th>
          <th scope="col">Behaviour</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">15 and earlier</th>
          <td>
            Logical slots can only be created on the primary, and are not
            propagated to replicas. After a failover the new primary has no
            slot, so treat it as a lost slot: re-snapshot. Debezium's
            <code>snapshot.mode=always</code> is documented for exactly the case
            where a new primary was promoted.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">16</th>
          <td>
            Logical slots can be created on a standby, but Debezium's docs say
            the standby's slot is not synchronised automatically and should be
            advanced manually to match the primary.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">17 and later</th>
          <td>
            A slot created with <code>failover = true</code> (the fifth argument
            of <code>pg_create_logical_replication_slot</code>; Debezium's
            <code>slot.failover</code>) can be synchronised to a standby, so the
            consumer can continue from the new primary after promotion.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Failover slots are not a setting you flip on a slot. For synchronisation to
      work on PostgreSQL 17 and later, the PostgreSQL docs require:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>sync_replication_slots = on</code> on the standby (it is off by
        default), a physical slot between primary and standby named in
        <code>primary_slot_name</code>, <code>hot_standby_feedback</code> enabled,
        and a valid <code>dbname</code> in <code>primary_conninfo</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Strongly recommended: list that physical slot in
        <code>synchronized_standby_slots</code> on the primary, so a subscriber
        cannot consume changes faster than the standby. The price is latency:
        logical senders wait for the standby to confirm, and the primary will not
        shut down completely until the listed standbys confirm.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Caveats worth knowing before you trust it:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The standby persists a synchronised slot only if the WAL and catalog rows
        it needs are still available on the standby. Otherwise it logs that
        synchronisation could lead to data loss and does not persist the slot.
        Temporary synchronised slots cannot be used for logical decoding.
      </li>
      <li>
        Verify readiness before you rely on it. The PostgreSQL docs give this
        check on the standby (<code>synced</code> and not temporary and not
        invalidated):
        <pre><code>SELECT slot_name,
       (synced AND NOT temporary AND invalidation_reason IS NULL) AS failover_ready
FROM pg_replication_slots
WHERE synced;</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        A slot with no active consumer will not advance, which stalls
        synchronisation; the docs suggest advancing it manually on the primary.
      </li>
      <li>
        If the old primary comes back during promotion, subscribers can keep
        talking to it. The docs recommend disabling subscriptions before
        promotion and re-enabling them once the connection string points at the
        new primary.
      </li>
      <li>
        Synchronisation is periodic. My reading, not a documented guarantee: the
        position on the promoted standby can be behind what the consumer had
        confirmed, so expect some replay after a failover. That is the same
        at-least-once rule, not a new failure mode.
      </li>
      <li>
        Debezium: do not drop or re-create a failover slot, and pause the
        connector until you have verified the slot is intact. Some managed
        services (Debezium names AWS RDS and Google Cloud SQL) replicate the
        slot with disk replication instead, so check what yours does.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="decision" aria-labelledby="decision-title">
    <h2 id="decision-title">Decision table: what is wrong, and what to do</h2>
    <p>
      Take two samples of the triage query, far enough apart that
      <code>pg_current_wal_lsn()</code> has clearly advanced, and read them
      against this table. Dropping a slot is the last row you should reach for,
      not the first.
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>Slot situations, how to recognise them, and the recovery path</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Situation</th>
          <th scope="col">How you tell</th>
          <th scope="col">Recovery path</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Lagging consumer</th>
          <td>
            <code>active = true</code>; <code>confirmed_flush_lsn</code> is
            moving between samples but <code>unconfirmed</code> and
            <code>restart_lag</code> are growing or not shrinking;
            <code>wal_status</code> is <code>reserved</code> or
            <code>extended</code>.
          </td>
          <td>
            Fix throughput: the sink, the Connect task, the network, or a
            snapshot hogging the connector. Leave the slot alone. It costs disk
            until the consumer catches up; watch <code>safe_wal_size</code> if
            you set a limit. See
            <a href="{{ '/observability/' | url }}">Observability</a> and
            <a href="{{ '/ops-offsets/' | url }}">Ops: Offsets &amp; Replays</a>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Dead consumer, slot still healthy</th>
          <td>
            <code>active = false</code>; <code>confirmed_flush_lsn</code> does
            not move between samples; <code>wal_status</code> is not
            <code>lost</code>.
          </td>
          <td>
            Restart the connector. It resumes from the slot, and some changes
            will be delivered again, so the sink must already be idempotent
            (<a href="#replay">below</a>). If it cannot come back before the
            disk fills, extend the volume. Do not lower
            <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> to &ldquo;buy time&rdquo;: a
            limit below the slot's current <code>restart_lag</code> makes the
            slot <code>unreserved</code>, and it is invalidated at the next
            checkpoint, which moves you to the invalidated-slot row.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Abandoned slot</th>
          <td>
            <code>active = false</code> for a long time and no running
            connector, pipeline or product claims the <code>slot_name</code>.
          </td>
          <td>
            Confirm ownership, then drop it (<a href="#drop">next section</a>).
            Nothing is lost if it really is abandoned. If you are wrong, you
            have just created the last row for someone's live pipeline.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Invalidated slot</th>
          <td>
            <code>wal_status = 'lost'</code> or, on 17+,
            <code>invalidation_reason IS NOT NULL</code> (for example
            <code>wal_removed</code>, or <code>idle_timeout</code> on 18).
          </td>
          <td>
            The position is gone. Stop the connector, drop the slot, discard the
            old offset, and re-snapshot into the idempotent sink; then
            reconcile. A snapshot copies the rows that exist now, so rows
            deleted while the slot was lost stay in a sink that you did not
            rebuild, until a diff finds them. See
            <a href="{{ '/snapshotting/' | url }}">Snapshotting</a> and
            <a href="{{ '/reconciliation-surgery/' | url }}">Reconciliation &amp; Offset Surgery</a>.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Do not restart the connector against a fresh slot while keeping the old
      offset. Debezium's own upgrade procedure explains why: a new slot can only
      return changes from after it was created, so when the connector asks for
      the stored offset it can skip the existing change events and resume from
      the newest position, silently, with no records for what it skipped. Debezium
      documents a manual procedure for a rebuilt slot (rename the connector,
      remove old offsets, recreate the slot and publication, choose a snapshot
      mode); follow the version of that procedure that matches your Debezium.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="drop" aria-labelledby="drop-title">
    <h2 id="drop-title">Dropping an abandoned slot safely</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Identify the owner.</strong> Match <code>slot_name</code>,
        <code>plugin</code> and <code>database</code> against your connector
        configs (<code>slot.name</code>), your managed ELT products and your
        runbooks. If you cannot find an owner, ask before dropping.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Stop the consumer first.</strong> Dropping an active slot raises
        an error by default; the replication-protocol command has a
        <code>WAIT</code> option that waits instead. Check
        <code>active = false</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Write down what you are giving up.</strong> Save the row from
        the triage query (<code>restart_lsn</code>,
        <code>confirmed_flush_lsn</code>, <code>wal_status</code>). It is the
        evidence of how far the pipeline had got.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Drop it.</strong>
        <pre><code>SELECT pg_drop_replication_slot('my_abandoned_slot');</code></pre>
        The function drops a physical or logical slot and is the same as the
        replication command <code>DROP_REPLICATION_SLOT</code>. Re-run the triage
        query to confirm it is gone, then watch the WAL directory size over the
        following checkpoints rather than expecting an instant change.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Clean up the consumer side.</strong> If the pipeline is retired,
        delete the connector and its offsets, drop its publication and heartbeat
        table, and remove it from monitoring. If the pipeline continues, you are
        in the invalidated-slot row: the old offset is not safe to reuse, and a
        re-snapshot is the way back.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="muted small">
      Dropping is a decision about the pipeline's history, not a rule you apply
      at a given size. &ldquo;Drop the slot when it exceeds N GB&rdquo; is how a
      live pipeline ends up in the invalidated-slot row.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="replay" aria-labelledby="replay-title">
    <h2 id="replay-title">After a restart: replay, duplicates and the idempotent sink</h2>
    <p>
      The slot confirms what the connector received. Debezium publishes to Kafka
      and Kafka Connect commits offsets on its own schedule; Debezium's docs tie
      the two together, noting that when no offsets are committed the connector
      has no opportunity to send the latest LSN to the database. So there is
      always a window of changes that were delivered downstream but not yet
      confirmed to the slot.
      A restart, a crash (the slot may return to its last checkpointed position)
      or a failover sends them again. Debezium documents this as at-least-once
      delivery.
    </p>
    <p>
      The fix lives in the sink, not in Postgres:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Key the target on the <strong>primary key</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Carry the change's <strong>log position</strong> (for Postgres,
        Debezium's <code>source.lsn</code>) into the target, and apply a change
        only when its position is newer than the one stored for that key. A
        replay then does nothing, and an older change arriving after a newer one
        cannot overwrite it. Do not order by an event timestamp.
      </li>
      <li>
        Keep deletes as delete markers with their own log position, so a replayed
        insert cannot resurrect a deleted row.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The worked patterns are on
      <a href="{{ '/partitioning/#recon' | url }}">Partitioning: version guards and watermarks</a>,
      <a href="{{ '/materialization/' | url }}">Materialization 101</a> and
      <a href="{{ '/snapshotting/#idempotent' | url }}">Snapshotting: idempotent apply</a>.
      They matter doubly after slot loss: a re-snapshot is the one time rows
      from two different eras meet in the sink, and a position guard is what
      lets the snapshot and the stream converge. The related glossary entries
      are <a href="{{ '/glossary/#idempotent-write' | url }}">idempotent write</a>
      and <a href="{{ '/glossary/#effectively-once' | url }}">effectively-once</a>.
    </p>
    <aside class="callout" aria-label="No end-to-end exactly-once">
      <p>
        <strong>There is no exactly-once across the whole path.</strong> A slot
        gives the connector each change at least once. Whatever a particular
        component promises about exactly-once applies to its own hop; the
        pipeline as a whole is at-least-once delivery plus an idempotent sink,
        which gets you the same final state. See
        <a href="{{ '/exactly-once/' | url }}">Exactly-Once Semantics</a>.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section id="try-it" aria-labelledby="try-it-title">
    <h2 id="try-it-title">Try a re-insert in the playground</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="{{ '/playground/' | url }}">Change Feed Playground</a> has a
      <strong>Re-insert after Update</strong> template. It is not a replay: an
      earlier version of a row is written again as an ordinary later source
      write, and the sink applies it in order. No old event is delivered a
      second time, so it cannot show a stale change losing to a newer one; for
      that, see the guarded-apply examples <a href="#replay">above</a>. Pick it
      from the template list to see how each capture method reports that write.
      The playground is a simulation with made-up data; it has no Postgres
      slots.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="alerting" aria-labelledby="alerting-title">
    <h2 id="alerting-title">Alerting: policy, not magic numbers</h2>
    <p>
      Any threshold quoted without the database's write rate and the volume's
      free space is a guess. Write the alerts as rules, then derive the numbers
      from your own measurements. To measure the write rate, take the WAL
      position twice and difference it:
    </p>
    <pre><code>-- psql: remember the position now ...
SELECT pg_current_wal_lsn() AS t0 \gset
-- ... and later, bytes of WAL written since then
SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), :'t0')) AS written_since_t0;</code></pre>
    <table>
      <caption>Alert rules and what each one is derived from</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Alert when</th>
          <th scope="col">Derive the threshold from</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">A slot has had no active consumer for longer than the pipeline can tolerate</th>
          <td>
            That pipeline's recovery objective: time to notice, restart and
            catch up. Use <code>inactive_since</code> on 17+, or two samples of
            <code>active</code> on older versions.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Estimated time-to-full for the WAL volume is shorter than on-call response time</th>
          <td>
            Free space divided by the measured WAL growth rate, including the
            WAL a stalled slot is pinning. This is the one that keeps the
            primary up.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>wal_status</code> leaves <code>reserved</code>; page on <code>unreserved</code>, treat <code>lost</code> as an incident</th>
          <td>
            Your <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> and your WAL rate.
            <code>safe_wal_size</code> is the countdown; alert at a fraction of
            the limit chosen so someone can act before it reaches zero.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>confirmed_flush_lsn</code> has not moved over a window while <code>pg_current_wal_lsn()</code> has</th>
          <td>
            A window several times longer than your heartbeat interval (or, if
            you run no heartbeats, longer than your normal quiet period). It
            catches both a stuck consumer and a quiet table with no heartbeat.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Heartbeat records stop arriving at the sink</th>
          <td>
            The heartbeat interval, if you use heartbeats. It tells you the whole
            path is alive, not only the connector.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>age(catalog_xmin)</code> keeps rising on a slot</th>
          <td>
            The same guardrails you already use for transaction ID wraparound on
            this cluster.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Wire these into the dashboards and routes described on
      <a href="{{ '/observability/' | url }}">Observability Basics</a>, and link
      each alert to the matching row of the decision table above.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="checked" aria-labelledby="checked-title">
    <h2 id="checked-title">What was checked, and what was not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Read in the primary docs on 2026-10-09:</strong> the
        PostgreSQL pages for <code>pg_replication_slots</code> (14, 16, 17, 18),
        replication configuration (13, 16, 17, 18), logical decoding concepts
        (17), the replication protocol (17), the administration functions (17),
        logical replication failover (17) and the PostgreSQL 17 release notes,
        plus the Debezium PostgreSQL connector reference (stable).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Run:</strong> the monitoring queries were executed on PGlite
        0.5.8, which embeds PostgreSQL 18.3, with <code>wal_level = logical</code>
        and a real <code>pgoutput</code> slot that nothing consumed. They parse,
        plan and return the columns shown. On that instance, with
        <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size = 32MB</code>, writing about 50 MB of WAL
        took the slot from <code>reserved</code> (<code>safe_wal_size</code>
        41 MB) to <code>unreserved</code> (<code>safe_wal_size</code> had gone
        negative, -10048 kB), and a <code>CHECKPOINT</code> then moved it to
        <code>lost</code> with NULL <code>restart_lsn</code> and
        <code>safe_wal_size</code> and <code>invalidation_reason =
        'wal_removed'</code>. Peeking at the lost slot failed with &ldquo;can no
        longer access replication slot&rdquo;, and
        <code>pg_drop_replication_slot</code> removed it. With
        <code>idle_replication_slot_timeout = 1s</code> an unused slot became
        <code>lost</code> with <code>invalidation_reason = 'idle_timeout'</code>
        after a <code>CHECKPOINT</code>, and its <code>restart_lsn</code> stayed
        set; the triage query shows a NULL <code>restart_lag</code> for it. The
        heartbeat table DDL, the action query and
        <code>ALTER PUBLICATION ... ADD TABLE</code> also ran.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not run:</strong> an actively streaming consumer. Decoding
        pending changes in PGlite (<code>pg_logical_slot_peek_binary_changes</code>
        with <code>pgoutput</code>) returned nothing and the instance then
        stopped returning rows, so <code>active</code> and
        <code>active_pid</code> were only checked as columns and never seen as
        <code>true</code>; PostgreSQL 14 to 17 behaviour (docs only), failover
        slots and synchronisation, the Debezium JSON, whether Debezium's
        <code>filtered</code> mode really removes a manually added heartbeat
        table, and any managed service (RDS, Cloud SQL) or the
        Fivetran product. Settings that depend on your Debezium release
        (<code>lsn.flush.mode</code>, <code>slot.failover</code>) are marked
        &ldquo;check your version&rdquo; for that reason.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

